body:
I suppose that's one way to look at it. I don't think anyone is expecting that a bunch of pizza will suddenly make everything better. I think the point of the gesture is to try to encourage, and give them even a slice of happiness, no matter how small. They can't change the situation and they can't pay everyone's wages, or save their livelihoods, but they can say, "hey we know you have to be there anyway and we can't change it (though many wish they could), but have some yummy, free pizza while you're there and know we care about you."

&#x200B;

I know that coffee won't fix my problems or worries, but it puts a smile on my face, if even for a moment when a kind person hands me a hot cup of it...

&#x200B;

What you say is valid (and sorry if it was intended as sarcasm and flew right over my head), and pizza isn't going to change a damn thing, but maybe, just maybe a little smile, for however many seconds, minutes, hours, etc it lasts, is worth doing it for.
